  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWeak waves will move through along an upper level trough today and \nMonday. These waves will be accompanied by enough moisture to \nsupport isolated to scattered showers. Some breaks in cloud cover \nand shower activity will allow for instability to increase and \nisolated thunderstorms to develop each afternoon. With weak flow \nabove, 0-6 km shear will be 20 kts or less, storms are expected to \nbe non-severe and short-lived. For those spending time outdoors, any \nstorms that develop may contain gusty winds and even small hail as \ninstability extends into the hail growth zone. For late today, PoPs \nwere added over the Catskills for late today as most guidance show \nthe potential for some diurnally driven showers to develop over that \nregion. Then on Monday, NBM had high PoPs for the Catskills and \nPoconos in an environment that will favor pulse showers and storms. \nAdjustments were made to lower PoPs over these regions. Clear skies, \nlight winds, and some moisture from showers will favor fog \ndevelopment late tonight into early Monday morning. The current \npattern over the country will allow for vertically integrated \nwildfire smoke to move over the region today and especially Monday. \nWith this smoke expected to be so high up, there should be little to \nno impact to air quality. \n\nThe upper level trough will dig over the region Tuesday. As it does, \na low pressure system will develop, though there remains some \nuncertainty on where it develops and tracks. The ECMWF develops the \nsurface low over the Great Lakes early Tuesday and pushes it \neastward. The GFS is slower to develop the low and has it along the \ncoast south of the region. Either way, this system will bring the \nnext chance for more widespread rain showers Tuesday through at \nleast Wednesday. If this system were to stay more north, strong \nsoutherly flow will advect in warmer air and lead to a more unstable \nenvironment Tuesday afternoon, especially in areas where rain is \nslower to develop. However, shear will still be weak so chances for \nstronger thunderstorms is low.\n\nWithin any thunderstorms, there will be potential for heavier \nrainfall rates. Otherwise, showers may become more stratiform \ndepending on the low's track. Models vary on rainfall amounts during \nthis timeframe though localized amounts of 1.5 to 2 inches are \npossible. Several dry days has resulted in soil moistures drier than \nnormal, so the region should be able to handle this system. WPC \nmaintains a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall over the region as \nthis system passes through.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe second half of the week remains more uncertain. The GFS has this \nsystem quickly exiting the region and replaced with high pressure \nand drier air. Meanwhile, the ECMWF has been consistent with having \nthis system linger over the Northeast with spotty showers Thursday \nthrough at least Saturday. Given this, this forecast update will \nmaintain low chances for showers each day. Instability would be more \nlimited, though isolated, afternoon thunderstorms cannot be ruled \nout. If this system lingers, then total rainfall amounts will \nincrease, potentially adding on another inch onto what will \nhave already fallen early in the week.",
